  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 PM EDT Mon Sep 14 2026\n\nVery pleasant temperatures in the 70s and dewpoints in the 40s will \nshift away as the sfc ridge moves east and an increasing low level \nflow starts ushering much higher dewpoints to the area. This will \ntake a bit of time, with values into the low to mid 50s by 12Z Tue. \nOff to our NW, an axis of much higher dewpoints will be pooled along \na slow moving frontal boundary that is expected to approach later \ntomorrow and linger across the region (in a weaker state) through \nThursday. Dewpoints and low level temps will climb through the day \nwith a much more muggy feel returning by afternoon. High temps \nbefore any convection occurs will reach well into the 80s for most \nlocations and if cloud cover stay away long enough, a few 90s are \npossible in SW areas. \n\nModels have generally backed off on upstream convection making it \ninto NW parts of the area as it encounters the drier air mass and \noutruns the better forcing. As we move into Tuesday afternoon and \nbeyond, this changes with at least chance pops warranted as leftover \noutflow boundaries that may drift in during peak heating interact \nwith increasing CAPE. There seems to be a continued lean towards \nbetter development in the 20 to 22Z time frame, mainly NW but \nhonestly could end up just about anywhere. These then progress \nsoutheast through about 2Z to 3Z Wed before shifting out of the area \nor weakening. Shear profiles are on the lower side (25 to maybe 35 \nkts) for some organization of convection, and decent low level lapse \nrates will help with the initial lift, but mid level lapse rates are \nquite unfavorable for more robust convection. SPC has maintained the \nMarginal Risk for severe storms with some gusty wind potential for \nthose storms that can interact with boundaries and maybe sustain \nsome briefly better updrafts and cores. \n\nBeyond 6Z Wed forecast becomes even more challenging as frontal \nboundary slowly weakens and stalls over the general area with models \nstruggling (as to be expected) with the handling of subtle waves and \npossible new development or propagation of upstream convection. \nGiven the amount of lingering moisture in the area and at least some \ninstability, it won't take much to pop additional showers and \nstorms, thus warranting the chc to likely pops through Thursday. By \nno means does it look like a washout with periods of little/no \nprecip likely in between waves, but no way to time those out at this \npoint in the forecast. Severe threat should be very minimal, if non \nexistent, but heavy rain/localized flooding could become an issue \nwith high PWATs and any repeated training of multiple rounds of \nconvection. Marginal risk for Excessive Rainfall remains in place \nTue and Wed and is reasonable.  \n\nNo changes beyond Thursday as models seem to want to push the \nridging north into MI for the weekend. However, history over the \npast several weeks in this setup has pointed towards low confidence \non how much of this comes to fruition. As a result, blend is left \nalone with some slgt chc to chc pops, especially Sat.",
